            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Exa API FAQ</h2>

            <div class="space-y-6">
              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-5">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is Exa in 2026?</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-700">Exa is an applied-AI research lab (Exa Labs, San Francisco) that runs its own web index and its own search models. The company has been building search infrastructure for AI systems since 2021; its search engine is purpose-built for AI-agent retrieval, not for human web search. Exa exposes six purpose-built endpoints (Search, Deep Search, Contents, Answer, Monitors, Agent), an official MCP server at mcp.exa.ai/mcp (4,987 GitHub stars on the self-hosted variant), and SDKs for Python (exa-py, 233 stars on GitHub) and JavaScript/TypeScript (exa-js). Verified live on September 8, 2026: exa.ai and api.exa.ai both serve HTTP 200; pricing page lists $7 per 1k Search requests, $12-$15 per 1k Deep Search requests, and a free tier of $20 signup credits plus $10 per month.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-5">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does Exa pricing work in 2026?</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-700">Pay-as-you-go with no subscription and no minimum spend. Search is $7 per 1,000 requests for the first 10 results (with $1 per 1k extra results and $1 per 1k AI page summaries on top). Deep Search runs $12-$15 per 1k by tier (deep-lite, deep, deep-reasoning). Contents is $1 per 1k pages. Answer is $5 per 1k. Monitors is $15 per 1k. Agent is $0.012 to $1.00 per fixed-effort run (minimal / low / medium / high / xhigh) or usage-based metered with a default $5 per-run cap. New accounts receive $20 in free credits at signup and $10 in free credits every month with no payment method required. Enterprise is custom via sales demo (volume discounts, custom indexes, Zero Data Retention, SLAs).</p>
              </div>

              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-5">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is Exa Dynamic Highlights?</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-700">Dynamic Highlights is the August 2026 research-preview release that selects excerpts across the complete result set instead of treating each page independently. The agent's token budget goes to useful sources and not to ones that repeat what other results already said. Opt in by setting dynamic: true on a Search or Contents request and adding the Exa-Beta: dynamic-highlights-2026-08-28 header. Exa's published benchmark: about 49% better token efficiency and +2.4% downstream quality for single-turn RAG; about 30% fewer tokens across agent trajectories and +1% quality across BrowseComp, WideSearch, and internal company / people evaluations. The benchmark harness is open source at exa-labs/benchmarks on GitHub.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-5">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Exa have an MCP server?</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — Exa ships both a hosted MCP server at https://mcp.exa.ai/mcp and a self-hosted MCP server from exa-labs/exa-mcp-server (4,987 GitHub stars, MIT license, last commit 2026-08-21). The hosted path lets you wire Exa into Claude Desktop, Cursor, Codex, VS Code (with the MCP-enabled extension), or any MCP-compatible client without running infrastructure. The self-hosted path is the right choice when you want to keep API keys inside your own infrastructure. Both expose /search, /get_contents, /research, and /company_research as MCP tools.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-5">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does Exa compare to Tavily and Firecrawl?</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-700">Exa, Tavily, and Firecrawl target different parts of the retrieval pipeline. Exa is the right choice for live web search and structured retrieval across web, company, person, and publication verticals; Tavily is the closest agent-builder competitor with hosted SaaS and a polished UI; Firecrawl is the right tool for crawling known sites and converting them to markdown. The pragmatic setup for many production agents is to pair Exa (live search) with Firecrawl (static ingestion) rather than pick one over the other. Against SerpAPI and Serper, Exa wins on agent-shaped features (Dynamic Highlights, MCP, structured outputs) but loses on raw SERP-rank checking where Google-shape results matter.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-5">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can Exa be used from mainland China?</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-700">There is no official mainland-China hosting region for exa.ai or api.exa.ai, so direct access depends on local ISP egress and the team's compliant network path. The product is API-only with no GUI requirement, so the typical pattern is to call Exa from a non-mainland worker or route via a compliant egress that meets data-residency requirements. For teams that need to keep every prompt byte inside China, the alternative is to pair Exa with a self-hosted crawler + embedding pipeline for the same verticals, but you lose the vendor-maintained index advantage.</p>
